    "dock": {
      "word": "DOCK",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A genus of plants (Rumex), some species of which are well-known weeds which have a long taproot and are difficult of extermination. yellow root, which that of other species is used medicinally as an astringent and tonic.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The solid part of an animal's tail, as distinguished from the hair; the stump of a tail; the part of a tail left after clipping or cutting. Grew.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A case of leather to cover the clipped or cut tail of a horse.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "to cut off, as the end of a thing; to curtail; to cut short; to clip; as, to dock the tail of a horse. His top was docked like a priest biforn. Chaucer.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To cut off a part from; to shorten; to deduct from; to subject to a deduction; as, to dock one's wages.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To cut off, bar, or destroy; as, to dock an entail.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "An artificial basin or an inclosure in connection with a harbor or river, -- used for the reception of vessels, and provided with gates for keeping in or shutting out the tide.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The slip or water way extending between two piers or projecting wharves, for the reception of ships; -- sometimes including the piers themselves; as, to be down on the dock.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The place in court where a criminal or accused person stands. Balance dock, a kind of floating dock which is kept level by pumping water out of, or letting it into, the compartments of side chambers. especially, one in the form of a chamber having walls and floor, often of masonry and communicating with deep water, but having appliances for excluding it; -- used in constructing or repairing ships. The name includes structures used for the examination, repairing, or building of vessels, as graving docks, floating docks, hydraulic docks, etc. floating, to lift a vessel out of water. the bottom, etc. water by hydraulic presses. materials, and all conveniences for the construction and repair of ships. or caissons. water to above high-water mark, and upon which is a railway on which runs a cradle carrying the ship. level, to facilitate the loading and unloading of ships; -- also sometimes used as a place of safety; a basin.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To draw, law, or place (a ship) in a dock, for repairing, cleaning the bottom, etc.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
