    "docket": {
      "word": "DOCKET",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A small piece of paper or parchment, containing the heads of a writing; a summary or digest.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A bill tied to goods, containing some direction, as the name of the owner, or the place to which they are to be sent; a label. Bailey.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Law) (a) An abridged entry of a judgment or proceeding in an action, or register or such entries; a book of original, kept by clerks of courts, containing a formal list of the names of parties, and minutes of the proceedings, in each case in court. (b) (U. S.) A list or calendar of causes ready for hearing or trial, prepared for the use of courts by the clerks.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A list or calendar of business matters to be acted on in any assembly. On the docket, in hand; in the plan; under consideration; in process of execution or performance. [Colloq.]",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To make a brief abstract of (a writing) and indorse it on the back of the paper, or to indorse the title or contents on the back of; to summarize; as, to docket letters and papers. Chesterfield.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Law) (a) To make a brief abstract of and inscribe in a book; as, judgments regularly docketed. (b) To enter or inscribe in a docket, or list of causes for trial.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To mark with a ticket; as, to docket goods.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
